{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי אם הלחם אשר הוא אוכל", :translit "ki im ha-lechem asher hu ochel", :gloss "for only the bread that he eats"} {:he "היא אשתו", :translit "hi ishto", :gloss "it is his wife"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "כי אם הלחם אשר הוא אוכל", :b "היא אשתו"}], :frame {:text "The verse states that Potiphar entrusted everything to Joseph, withholding nothing 'for only the bread that he eats.' Genesis 39:7 immediately follows: 'After these things, his master's wife cast her eyes upon Joseph.' The Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 87:4) interprets that Potiphar's wife looked at Joseph with intent to consort with him. Rashi on Genesis 39:11, citing the Talmud (Sotah 36b), explains that the word 'bread' in this verse is a euphemism: Potiphar ate 'bread' meaning he sought male relations, and therefore had no relations with his wife, which is why she turned to Joseph. The Ba'al HaTurim compresses this tradition into a numerical equation: the verse's seemingly innocuous phrase about 'bread' itself encodes the words 'she is his wife.'", :sources ["Genesis 39:7" "Genesis 39:11" "Rashi on Genesis 39:11" "Sotah 36b" "Genesis Rabbah 87:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:a-value 724, :b-value 723, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "היא אשתו", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "כי אם הלחם אשר הוא אוכל"}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:10:47.841926968Z", :model "glm-5.2"}